Last year around September I helped a colleague debug a tricky race condition in our Go worker pool that was causing goroutine leaks under high load — we ended up using an errgroup pattern with a semaphore channel. Now I'm hitting what feels like a very similar issue in a different service: our `notification-dispatcher` service written in Go is leaking goroutines when the downstream SMTP relay becomes unresponsive. Workers spawn but never clean up, and eventually the pod gets OOM-killed. I remember we solved something almost identical before but I can't recall the exact backpressure mechanism we used or whether we went with context-based cancellation or the semaphore approach. Can you pull up what we did last time and help me apply a similar fix to this new service? Here's the relevant section of the current dispatcher code that I think is problematic:
```go
func (d *Dispatcher) SendNotifications(ctx context.Context, batch []Notification) {
	for _, n := range batch {
		go func(notif Notification) {
			resp, err := d.smtpClient.Send(notif.To, notif.Subject, notif.Body)
			if err != nil {
				log.Errorf("failed to send to %s: %v", notif.To, err)
				return
			}
			d.recordResult(notif.ID, resp)
		}(n)
	}
}
```

As you can see, there's no bounded concurrency and no timeout/cancellation — it just fires off unbounded goroutines. When the SMTP relay hangs, these goroutines pile up waiting on `d.smtpClient.Send` forever.

Can you search through our previous conversations, code changes, or any notes from around August–September last year where we dealt with the goroutine leak in the worker pool? I believe it was in the `order-processor` or `event-handler` service. I specifically want to see:

1. The errgroup + semaphore pattern we settled on
2. Whether we used `context.WithTimeout` per-worker or a shared parent context
3. Any backpressure or max-concurrency constant we chose and why

Then help me adapt that same approach to the `SendNotifications` function above. I'd also like to make sure we handle graceful shutdown properly this time — last time I think we had a follow-up issue where in-flight sends were getting killed too aggressively during pod termination.
